+# Narrowing NG-9 to Admit Filtering the Veterinarian Directory
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+NG-9 declared searching for a pet, a visit, or a veterinarian out of scope. Only
+owners were searchable.
+
+The clinic now wants the veterinarian directory narrowed to a single specialty.
+The directory already shows each veterinarian's specialties, so the narrowing
+reads an attribute the page publishes rather than searching a hidden one. That
+request meets NG-9 head on and needs the non-goal settled before the capability
+is recorded.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Keep NG-9 whole** — refuse the request; only owners stay findable.
+2. **Retire NG-9** — open pet, visit, and veterinarian search alike.
+3. **Narrow NG-9** — free-text veterinarian search stays out; narrowing the
+   directory by an attribute it already shows comes in.
+
+## Decision
+
+We take option 3. NG-9 keeps free-text search for a pet, a visit, or a
+veterinarian out of scope, and admits narrowing a published list by a value that
+list already displays.
+
+Option 1 refuses a capability whose cost is bounded by an attribute already on
+the page. Option 2 gives up the boundary that keeps paged prefix search
+demonstrated once, in owner search.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The veterinarian directory gains a specialty filter, recorded as `REQ-VET-003`.
+- Free-text search over pets, visits, and veterinarians remains out of scope, so
+  owner search stays the single demonstration of paged prefix search.
+- The boundary now turns on a distinction a future request must be tested
+  against: filtering a displayed attribute against searching free text.
+- A later request to filter another published list has a precedent to cite,
+  which widens the surface one list at a time.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Non-goal:** NG-9
+
+## References
+
+- [PRD Non-Goals](../prd.md#non-goals)
+- [PRD Veterinarian directory](../prd.md#req-vet-003)
