+# Filtered Veterinarian Queries Stay Out of the Vets Cache
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+Both read methods on `VetRepository` are cached under a single cache named
+`vets`. `CacheConfiguration` declares that cache through the JCache programmatic
+API, which offers no size limit; the source comment records that a limit must
+come from the provider instead. No eviction is configured, and the application
+has no write path for veterinarians.
+
+`REQ-VET-003` adds a query whose argument is a specialty name taken from the
+request. Caching it would put caller-supplied text into the cache key.
+
+The existing page query already takes a caller-supplied page number into its
+key, so the key space is not bounded by the data today. A string argument widens
+that from small integers to text of any length and any cardinality.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Cache the filtered queries under `vets`** — symmetric with the two existing
+   methods, and the shape an implementer reaches for by default.
+2. **Cache them in a second, size-bounded cache** — keeps the hit rate and caps
+   the growth.
+3. **Leave the filtered queries uncached** — the unfiltered directory keeps its
+   cache; a narrowed request always reads the database.
+
+## Decision
+
+We take option 3. The methods that take a specialty name are not annotated
+`@Cacheable`; the two existing unfiltered methods keep their annotation
+unchanged.
+
+Option 1 lets any caller grow an unbounded cache without limit, by sending
+distinct specialty values. Option 2 needs a size limit that the JCache
+configuration API in this project cannot express, so it moves cache sizing into
+provider-specific configuration for one query. Neither cost is worth a hit rate
+on a three-row specialty list backed by an in-memory database.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A narrowed directory request reads the database every time. The unfiltered
+  directory is unaffected and stays cached.
+- The `vets` cache keeps a key space of pageables only, so no request text
+  reaches it.
+- Two methods on one repository are cached and two are not. The split is not
+  visible from the method names and depends on this record to be understood.
+- Should veterinarians ever grow to a size where the filtered query costs real
+  time, the decision is revisited rather than reversed by adding an annotation.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** [`REQ-VET-003`](../prd.md#req-vet-003) — narrowing either form
+of the veterinarian directory to one specialty.
+
+## References
+
+- [system-design.md § Contracts](../system-design.md#contracts) — the two
+  directory routes and the parameter they accept
+- [system-design.md § Open Questions from the Survey](../system-design.md#open-questions-from-the-survey)
+  — the unsettled question of vet cache invalidation
+- [Narrowing NG-9 to Admit Filtering the Veterinarian Directory](2026-08-07-non-goal-vet-specialty-filter.md)
+  — the product decision this query serves
