+### Veterinarian directory routes
+
+The directory is published at two routes. `/vets.html` renders the paged HTML page; `/vets` returns the serialized collection wrapped by `Vets`. Both accept an optional `specialty` request parameter naming one specialty (`REQ-VET-003`). A blank value is read as absent, as a blank owner search is, and the whole directory is returned.
+
+The name is matched whole and case-insensitively by the query itself, not by column collation. Owner search relies on collation instead and diverges by database vendor — see [Known Defects](#known-defects).
+
+Pagination links on the HTML page carry the parameter through Thymeleaf link parameters, which URL-encode the value. The expression-preprocessing form used by the unfiltered links is not applied to request-supplied text.
+
+The repository methods taking a specialty name are uncached; the unfiltered ones stay cached — see [ADR: Filtered Veterinarian Queries Stay Out of the Vets Cache](adr/2026-08-07-uncached-filtered-vet-queries.md).
+
 ### Persistence
 
 The relational schema is not generated from the JPA mapping. `spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto` is set to `none`, and the schema is created by the per-vendor `schema.sql` scripts under `src/main/resources/db/`, with seed rows from the matching `data.sql`. The domain classes carry the JPA mapping annotations directly — there is no separate persistence model and no mapper between them. Under `architecture-principles.md` this is the sanctioned direct-mapping case: the project owns both the model and the schema, and the stored shape tracks the model closely.
